    coverImagePosition: 50,
    // representative photo — a general view of the Yongsan district of Seoul, not the specific HYBE or Weverse office building
    body: `Weverse, the fan community and merchandise app built by HYBE Corporation — the entertainment company behind BTS, Le Sserafim, Enhypen, and dozens of other K-pop acts — disclosed on September 6, 2026 that a security vulnerability had exposed personal data tied to 422,584 user accounts. An outside tipster first flagged the flaw to the Korea Internet & Security Agency (KISA) on September 3, and Weverse says it locked down the vulnerability within days of being notified. The company traced the problem to an application programming interface (API) used to process payment information, which an outside party was able to query without proper authorization.

The exposed data was narrower than in many breaches of this size but still sensitive: internal account identifiers, along with purchase details including payment method type, transaction currency and amount, purchase and refund dates, and order status. Weverse says the leak did not include user names, phone numbers, email addresses, passwords, or complete card numbers, and that the specific fields exposed make direct payment forgery or unauthorized transfers unlikely using this data alone. That distinction matters, but it undersells why the breach is still worth fans' attention: Weverse isn't just a chat app for BTS and other HYBE acts, it's the primary storefront where a global fanbase — heavily weighted toward the United States, Latin America, Japan, and Southeast Asia — buys albums, merchandise, and paid content, and purchase history on the platform can double as an entry list for "fansign" lottery events, where the number of albums a fan has bought affects their odds of winning a video call or in-person meeting with an artist. A leak touching purchase amounts and timing, even without a name attached, is exactly the kind of detail a scammer could pair with other information to make a follow-up phishing message feel credible.

HYBE Weverse Company CEO Yang Joo-il issued a formal apology to users on the night of September 6, and the company says it has restricted access to the vulnerable API, removed the exposed internal identifiers, and committed to auditing all of its externally accessible APIs going forward. If you have a Weverse account, change your password and enable two-factor authentication if you haven't already, and review your linked payment methods and recent purchase or subscription history for anything you don't recognize. Because transaction details were exposed, treat any message claiming to be from Weverse about a "refund," "payment issue," or "order verification" with real suspicion in the weeks ahead — log into the app or go to weverse.io directly rather than clicking a link in an email or text, and never enter a card number, password, or one-time code into a page you reached through an unsolicited message. Suspected phishing referencing this breach can be reported to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov, and international fans can also check their own country's consumer-protection agency for local reporting options.`,
