    coverImagePosition: 50,
    // representative photo — a generic döner kebab restaurant in Turkey, not a Baydöner location specifically
    body: `Baydöner, a well-known Turkish restaurant and döner kebab chain, had its customer database posted to a hacking forum in March 2026, exposing records tied to roughly 1.3 million unique email addresses. Turkey's data protection authority, the Personal Data Protection Authority (KVKK), later investigated and traced the intrusion back to compromised login credentials belonging to a company employee, which attackers used to get into Baydöner's information systems. In early September 2026, KVKK announced the results of that investigation along with a formal fine against the chain.

What makes this breach unusually serious is not just its size but what was actually stored in plain view. Alongside names, phone numbers, cities of residence, dates of birth, and genders, the leaked database included roughly 622,000 customer passwords stored in plaintext — meaning they were never scrambled with encryption or hashing in the first place, so anyone who obtained the database could read them directly, no cracking required. A smaller number of records also included Turkish national ID numbers and purchase history tied to the chain's loyalty program. Baydöner has said payment and financial data were not affected, and KVKK's investigation reportedly traced part of the incident back to a security lapse at a third-party vendor from as early as August 2025. Regulators fined the company for failing to maintain adequate data security measures and for gaps in how it notified those affected.

Plaintext password storage matters well beyond Baydöner's own app, because of one of the most common security mistakes people make: reusing the same password across multiple accounts. If you had a Baydöner account and used that same password anywhere else — a banking app, an email account, another delivery or loyalty app — a criminal who now has that exact password in readable form can simply try it on those other accounts, a technique called credential stuffing that succeeds far more often than most people expect. The same is true for the exposed national ID numbers, which in Turkey function similarly to a Social Security number in the U.S. and can be used to attempt identity theft or fraudulent account openings.

If you have ever had a Baydöner account, change that password immediately, and — more importantly — change it anywhere else you may have reused it, prioritizing your email and banking logins first since those are the highest-value targets. Use a unique password for every account going forward, ideally generated and stored by a password manager, so a breach at any one company can't cascade into others. Watch for phishing messages that reference your name, phone number, or past Baydöner orders to appear more credible, and never respond to a text or call asking you to "verify" your national ID number or a one-time code. Turkish consumers can check whether their information was exposed and file complaints through KVKK at kvkk.gov.tr; those elsewhere who reused a Baydöner password can check exposure at a service like Have I Been Pwned and should still change any reused credentials as a precaution.`,
